virglrenderer: Venus primaries hold the secondaries they executed

What: a primary command buffer holds each secondary vkCmdExecuteCommands named until the
driver let go of what it recorded (a reset, a new begin, a free, a pool reset or destroy,
the context's end), as it holds the views its passes named (virgl-venus-command-holds.patch).
The app may free a held secondary or destroy its pool (the primary becomes invalid, which
Vulkan allows): its id leaves vkr's table at once and it stays in the driver, a zombie that
holds its pool, until the last primary lets go; then it is freed into its pool. A destroyed
pool with such a zombie leaves the table too, its other command buffers are freed one by
one first, and the driver's destroy waits for its last zombie. Without held command
buffers a pool goes at once, as before. The device's teardown destroys its pools the same
way and frees zombies left over holders first.
Why: MoltenVK's vkCmdExecuteCommands keeps the secondaries' pointers (MVKCmdExecuteCommands,
MVKCmdPipeline.h) and reads them when it encodes the primary at vkQueueSubmit
(MVKCmdPipeline.mm); a destroyed pool deletes its command buffers, so a guest app could make
QEMU read freed memory (#676, from #673's review). KosmicKrisp replays a secondary into the
primary while recording.
Upstream: OmacVM policy (object lifetime: VUID-vkCmdExecuteCommands-commonparent, invalid
primaries).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-venus-commands.c (the c-state-secondary-held- cases, also under
libgmalloc).

